<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>The Supplier Blindspot — approach, solution and assumptions</title>
<style>
  @page { size: A4; margin: 16mm 16mm 15mm; }
  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  html { -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact; print-color-adjust: exact; }
  body { margin: 0; font: 9.6pt/1.5 "Segoe UI", system-ui, Arial, sans-serif; color: #17191e; }
  h1 { font-size: 21pt; line-height: 1.15; margin: 0 0 4pt; letter-spacing: -.015em; }
  h2 { font-size: 12.5pt; margin: 18pt 0 5pt; padding-top: 6pt; border-top: 1.5px solid #17191e; break-after: avoid; }
  h3 { font-size: 10pt; margin: 10pt 0 3pt; break-after: avoid; }
  p { margin: 0 0 6pt; }
  ul, ol { margin: 0 0 6pt; padding-left: 14pt; }
  li { margin: 1.5pt 0; }
  .muted { color: #5b6170; }
  .small { font-size: 8.4pt; }
  code { font-family: Consolas, "JetBrains Mono", monospace; font-size: 8.4pt; background: #f1f2f5; padding: 0 3pt; border-radius: 2pt; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 4pt 0 8pt; font-size: 8.6pt; break-inside: avoid; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 3pt 5pt; border-bottom: 1px solid #e3e5ea; vertical-align: top; }
  th { font-size: 7.6pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .05em; color: #5b6170; border-bottom: 1.5px solid #17191e; }
  td.n, th.n { text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  tr.hl td { background: #fdf0f0; }
  .kpis { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 6pt; margin: 10pt 0; }
  .kpi { border: 1px solid #d9dce3; border-radius: 5pt; padding: 6pt 8pt; }
  .kpi .l { font-size: 7.2pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; color: #5b6170; }
  .kpi .v { font-size: 13pt; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 1pt; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .kpi .s { font-size: 7.8pt; color: #3d4250; }
  .box { border-left: 3px solid #17191e; background: #f6f7f9; padding: 6pt 9pt; margin: 6pt 0 8pt; break-inside: avoid; }
  .two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 14pt; }
  .meta { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 8.4pt; color: #5b6170; border-bottom: 2px solid #17191e; padding-bottom: 6pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
{% set a = s.attribution %}{% set b = s.bottom %}{% set v = s.validation %}
<div class="meta"><span>MCCIA Applied AI Studio · PS 4 — The Supplier Blindspot</span><span>Approach, solution and assumptions</span></div>
<h1>Finding the three suppliers who cost Arora Traders {{ b.total_impact | lakh }} — and putting the proof on paper</h1>
<p class="muted">{{ "{:,}".format(s.counts.orders) }} purchase orders, {{ s.counts.returns }} customer returns and {{ s.counts.suppliers }} suppliers, {{ s.period.start }} to {{ s.period.end }}. Every figure in this document is generated by the same pipeline run as the web app and the briefs.</p>

<div class="kpis">
  <div class="kpi"><div class="l">Short delivery + returns</div><div class="v">{{ s.totals.rubric_core_total | lakh }}</div><div class="s">All suppliers, the problem statement’s formula</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="l">Bottom three, total</div><div class="v">{{ b.total_impact | lakh }}</div><div class="s">{{ b.share_of_impact | pct(0) }} of all losses on {{ b.share_of_spend | pct(0) }} of spend</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="l">Untraced returns</div><div class="v">{{ a.metrics.misallocation | pct }}</div><div class="s">value misallocated (vs {{ a.baselines.most_recent_batch.misallocation | pct(0) }} for the stated rule)</div></div>
  {% if v.available and v.flagged %}<div class="kpi"><div class="l">Blind check</div><div class="v">{{ v.hits | length }} / {{ v.flagged | length }}</div><div class="s">bottom three found without the answer key</div></div>
  {% else %}<div class="kpi"><div class="l">Blind check</div><div class="v">n/a</div><div class="s">no suppliers flagged in the answer key</div></div>{% endif %}
</div>

<h2>1 · What was asked, and where each answer lives</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Deliverable</th><th>Where</th><th>In one line</th></tr>
  <tr><td>D1 · Cleaning + attribution</td><td><code>pipeline/load.py</code>, <code>prepare.py</code>, <code>attribute.py</code></td><td>Files identified by column signature, typed, joined with row-count assertions; {{ s.counts.returns_blank }} untraced returns attributed by a validated model.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>D2 · Scorecard</td><td>Web app → Overview; <code>scorecard.csv</code> written by the pipeline</td><td>Four dimensions, evidence-weighted, 0–100, per supplier and per supplier × material.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>D3 · ₹ impact</td><td>Web app → each supplier; <code>money.py</code></td><td>Problem-statement formula verbatim, then clearly separated additional losses.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>D4 · Briefs</td><td><code>web/public/briefs/*.pdf</code></td><td>Five two-page A4 briefs: numbered asks, arithmetic, evidence POs, expected pushback.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>D5 · Replace 3</td><td>Web app → Overview</td><td>{{ b.suppliers | join(", ") }}, with the best-scoring existing alternatives per material.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>2 · What the data actually contains</h2>
<p>Before modelling, a pre-flight script (<code>python -m pipeline.preflight</code>) tested every assumption the arithmetic depends on. The findings that changed the design:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>The answer key is in the data.</b> <code>supplier_master.is_underperformer</code> flags {{ v.flagged | join(", ") }}. It is removed at load time and kept sealed; no scoring or modelling code can reach it. It is opened once, after the ranking is final, to check the result (section 6).</li>
  <li><b>Billing is on ordered quantity</b> on 100% of rows, so <code>invoice_amount_billed − quantity_received × unit_price_quoted</code> equals the value of material billed but not delivered. The rubric’s formula and short-delivery loss are the same number.</li>
  <li><b>The market index cannot judge individual suppliers.</b> It covers {{ s.index_context | length }} of {{ s.counts.materials }} materials, and where it exists the whole panel pays well above it (SS Pipes is on a different scale entirely). Price premium is measured against what other suppliers on the panel charged for the same material in the same quarter; the index is quoted only as context.</li>
  <li><b>Unit prices are noisy</b> — every material spans roughly ₹45,000–₹1,25,000/MT — so no supplier-level premium survives a significance test (section 4).</li>
  <li><b>The PS’s suggested attribution rule does not fit the labelled data.</b> On returns whose supplier <i>is</i> recorded, that supplier is rarely the one whose batch of that material arrived most recently. Section 5 replaces it with a model and shows the difference.</li>
  <li>Smaller issues, handled and logged: <code>days_late</code> in payments does not reconcile with its own dates (recomputed); the year in <code>po_id</code> disagrees with <code>po_date</code> on most rows (time grouping uses <code>po_date</code>); {{ s.counts.returns_blank }} of {{ s.counts.returns }} returns ({{ (s.counts.returns_blank / s.counts.returns) | pct(0) }}) are untraced, close to the stated 35%.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3 · Financial quantification (40%)</h2>
<p>Per purchase order, in rupees, then summed. Two tiers so a reviewer checking the stated formula finds exactly that number:</p>
<div class="box">
  <b>A · Short delivery</b> = Σ ( invoice_amount_billed − quantity_received × unit_price_quoted )<br>
  <b>B · Customer returns</b> = Σ quantity_returned × the supplier’s own median price for that material × P(supplier)<br>
  <span class="muted">P = 1 for a recorded return; the model’s probability for an untraced one. Each returned tonne is allocated exactly once.</span><br>
  <b>Headline (the rubric’s figure) = A + B.</b> &nbsp; Additional, shown separately: material rejected at our inspection (rejected MT × quoted price), return handling ({{ (cfg.RETURN_HANDLING_FACTOR*100)|round|int }}% of return value — an assumption), and price premium only if statistically established.
</div>
<table>
  <tr><th>Supplier</th><th class="n">Rank</th><th class="n">A · Short</th><th class="n">B · Returns</th><th class="n">A + B</th><th class="n">Rejected</th><th class="n">Handling</th><th class="n">Total</th></tr>
  {% for r in suppliers[:6] %}
  <tr class="{{ 'hl' if r.supplier_id in b.suppliers else '' }}"><td>{{ r.supplier_name }} <span class="muted">{{ r.supplier_id }}</span></td><td class="n">{{ r.rank }}</td><td class="n">{{ r.short_loss | inr }}</td><td class="n">{{ r.return_loss | inr }}</td><td class="n"><b>{{ r.rubric_core_total | inr }}</b></td><td class="n">{{ r.reject_loss | inr }}</td><td class="n">{{ r.handling_loss | inr }}</td><td class="n">{{ r.total_impact | inr }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
  <tr><td colspan="4"><b>All {{ s.counts.suppliers }} suppliers</b></td><td class="n"><b>{{ s.totals.rubric_core_total | inr }}</b></td><td class="n">{{ s.totals.reject_loss | inr }}</td><td class="n">{{ s.totals.handling_loss | inr }}</td><td class="n">{{ s.totals.total_impact | inr }}</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Sanity check against the problem statement.</b> The PS estimates ₹8–12L a year lost to the three underperformers on ₹70–85L monthly procurement. The supplied data transacts {{ s.counts.monthly_spend | lakh }} a month, so absolute figures are larger. Measured as a rate — what the bottom three leak <i>beyond</i> the rest of the panel’s rate on the same spend — they lose {{ b.excess_rate_of_spend | pct(2) }} of spend. Applied to the stated procurement that is <b>{{ b.scaled_to_ps_low | lakh }} – {{ b.scaled_to_ps_high | lakh }} a year</b>, inside the PS’s own range, reached independently.</p>

<h2>4 · Scorecard (D2)</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Dimension</th><th class="n">Weight</th><th>Measure</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Short delivery</td><td class="n">{{ (s.weights.short_delivery*100)|round|int }}%</td><td>Quantity not delivered ÷ quantity ordered</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Quality rejection</td><td class="n">{{ (s.weights.quality_rejection*100)|round|int }}%</td><td>(Rejected at our inspection + customer returns attributed) ÷ quantity received</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Late delivery</td><td class="n">{{ (s.weights.late_delivery*100)|round|int }}%</td><td>Average days late per order. Nearly every supplier is a day or two late on most orders, so “% late” barely separates them; it is still reported.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Price premium</td><td class="n">{{ (s.weights.price_premium*100)|round|int }}%</td><td>Average premium over other suppliers, same material and quarter</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
  <li><b>Scored against the typical supplier, not the worst.</b> Each dimension scores 100 at or better than the panel median, falling to 0 at {{ cfg.ZERO_AT_UNITS|round|int }} robust standard deviations worse, where a unit is never smaller than a commercially material difference ({{ cfg.MATERIALITY.short_delivery }} pp short, {{ cfg.MATERIALITY.late_delivery }} days late, {{ cfg.MATERIALITY.quality_rejection }} pp quality, {{ cfg.MATERIALITY.price_premium }} pp price). Scaling to the panel’s best and worst would guarantee someone scores 0 even when nobody is bad; this does not (see section 9, “Tested on data with a known answer”).</li>
  <li><b>Fair to small suppliers.</b> Each rate is shrunk toward the panel average by (n·rate + k·panel) ÷ (n + k), k = {{ s.config.shrinkage_k }} orders, so a handful of bad orders cannot top the worst list. Price premium is shrunk by its own noise (James–Stein): the spread between suppliers is no larger than order-to-order noise would produce, so the dimension carries little weight in practice — which is the honest outcome.</li>
  <li><b>Price premium is never charged without evidence.</b> One-sided t-test per supplier, Benjamini–Hochberg correction across all {{ s.counts.suppliers }}. {% if s.premium.significant %}Established for {{ s.premium.significant | join(", ") }}.{% else %}None survives at {{ (s.premium.fdr*100)|round|int }}% FDR, so ₹0 is claimed. The briefs still ask for price alignment on materials where the supplier’s own median sits above the panel’s — a request backed by their orders, not a loss claim.{% endif %}</li>
  <li><b>Per material, not just per supplier.</b> Every metric is also computed per supplier × material (shrunk toward that supplier’s own rate) and flagged where it is at least {{ cfg.CATEGORY_FLAG_MULTIPLE|round|int }}× the panel’s rate for that material. These drive the brief’s material-specific asks and the D5 alternatives.</li>
  <li><b>Relationship length is not an input.</b> No scoring code reads <code>years_of_relationship</code>; a test shuffles it and asserts every score is unchanged.</li>
  <li><b>Robust to the weights.</b> The bottom three are identical under all {{ s.stability.scenarios | length }} weightings tested (base, equal, money-heavy, service-heavy, price-heavy).</li>
</ul>

<h2>5 · Missing-attribution inference (35%)</h2>
<p>A return records material, date, quantity and reason — but no PO or batch — so it cannot be joined to a delivery. It is treated as a ranking problem: <i>of all suppliers who had delivered before this return, how likely is each to be its source?</i></p>
<ol>
  <li><b>Candidates.</b> Every supplier with a delivery on or before the return date.</li>
  <li><b>Features</b>, each computed only from deliveries dated on or before the return (a test adds catastrophic future deliveries and asserts no feature changes):
    <ul>{% for k, label in a.feature_labels.items() %}<li>{{ label }} <span class="muted">(weight {{ '%+.2f'|format(a.coefficients[k]) }})</span></li>{% endfor %}</ul>
    The return-history feature uses labels, so under cross-validation it is rebuilt inside each fold from the training returns only.</li>
  <li><b>Model.</b> Logistic regression on (return, supplier) pairs, probabilities normalised per return, then blended with plain exposure (each supplier’s recent share of that material) at the weight held-out log loss prefers — {{ '%.2f'|format(a.metrics.blend_alpha) }} on the model for this data. It therefore cannot do worse than that simple rule, and falls back to it when the data has no learnable signal. {{ a.feature_labels | length + 1 }} parameters; fits in under a second; refits automatically on any new dataset.</li>
  <li><b>Validation.</b> {{ a.metrics.cv_folds }}-fold cross-validation grouped by return, on the {{ a.metrics.n }} returns whose supplier is recorded: hide the answer, predict, compare.</li>
</ol>
<table>
  <tr><th>Method</th><th class="n">Value misallocated ↓</th><th class="n">Right supplier in top 3 ↑</th><th class="n">Ranked first ↑</th></tr>
  <tr class="hl"><td><b>Our model</b></td><td class="n"><b>{{ a.metrics.misallocation | pct }}</b></td><td class="n"><b>{{ a.metrics.top3 | pct }}</b></td><td class="n"><b>{{ a.metrics.top1 | pct }}</b></td></tr>
  {% for k, x in a.baselines.items() %}<tr><td>{{ x.label }}</td><td class="n">{{ x.misallocation | pct }}</td><td class="n">{{ x.top3 | pct }}</td><td class="n">{{ x.top1 | pct }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</table>
<p><b>Why misallocation is the headline metric.</b> The rupee totals need each supplier’s <i>share</i> of return value to be right, not every individual return. Misallocation measures exactly that: the fraction of held-out return value that lands on the wrong supplier once summed per supplier. The model gets {{ (1 - a.metrics.misallocation) | pct(0) }} right; every rule-based alternative, including the PS’s, gets about half.</p>
<p><b>Honest limits.</b> Top-1 accuracy is capped by the data: several suppliers could have supplied any given return, and nothing on the return distinguishes them. The model’s confidence reflects this and is calibrated ({% for c in a.calibration %}{{ c.bin }}: predicted {{ c.predicted | pct(0) }}, observed {{ c.observed | pct(0) }}{{ '; ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}). That is why inferred returns are split by probability in the totals, shown as an estimate in the briefs, and <b>never demanded as a credit</b> — only recorded returns are.</p>
<p>With fewer than {{ cfg.MIN_LABELLED_FOR_TRAINING }} labelled returns the pipeline does not train; it falls back to the most-recent-batch rule and says so in every output (tested).</p>

<h2>6 · Blind validation</h2>
{% if v.available and v.flagged %}
<p>With the ranking frozen, the sealed <code>is_underperformer</code> column was opened for the first time. It flags {{ v.flagged | join(", ") }}. Our bottom three, computed without it: <b>{{ v.our_bottom | join(", ") }}</b> — ranks {% for k, r in v.flagged_ranks.items() %}{{ k }} #{{ r }}{{ ', ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} of {{ v.n_suppliers }}, {{ '%.0f'|format(v.gap_to_next) }} score points clear of the next supplier. The column was a check on the method, never an input to it.</p>
{% elif v.available %}
<p>The sealed <code>is_underperformer</code> column flags no supplier in this dataset, so there is nothing to validate against; the ranking stands on the evidence alone.</p>
{% else %}
<p>This dataset carries no answer-key column, so no blind check is possible; the ranking stands on the evidence alone.</p>
{% endif %}

<h2>7 · Negotiation briefs (25%) and recommendation</h2>
<p>Briefs are generated for the bottom {{ briefs | length }} by score. Each is two A4 pages: page one carries the rupee figure with its arithmetic, three or four numbered asks with the evidence for each, likely counter-arguments, and a walk-away position; page two lists the purchase orders to put on the table and a per-material breakdown. Rules: every ask cites a figure the supplier can check against their own invoice or our GRN; only recorded returns are claimed; the price ask quotes the panel median for the material. Example ({{ briefs[0].supplier_id }}): <i>“{{ briefs[0].asks[0].ask }}”</i></p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Replace</th><th class="n">Cost to us</th><th class="n">Avoidable by switching</th><th>Largest materials → best existing alternatives</th></tr>
  {% for r in s.replacements %}
  <tr><td>{{ r.supplier_name }} <span class="muted">{{ r.supplier_id }}</span></td><td class="n">{{ r.total_impact | lakh }}</td><td class="n">{{ r.avoidable_total | lakh }}</td>
    <td>{% for m in r.materials[:3] %}{{ m.material }} → {{ m.alternatives | map(attribute='supplier_name') | join(', ') }}{{ '; ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>

<h2>8 · Assumptions and limitations</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Return value uses the supplier’s own median price for the material returned; handling cost ({{ (cfg.RETURN_HANDLING_FACTOR*100)|round|int }}%) is an assumption and sits outside the headline.</li>
  <li>The late-delivery penalty in the briefs ({{ (cfg.LATE_PENALTY_PER_DAY*100)|round(1) }}%/day, capped at {{ (cfg.LATE_PENALTY_CAP*100)|round|int }}%) is a negotiating position; it is never added to any loss total.</li>
  <li>Arora’s own late payment (recomputed from invoice date + agreed days) is surfaced in each brief as the supplier’s likely counter-argument, not scored.</li>
  <li>Attribution assumes the labelled returns are representative of the unlabelled ones; nothing in the data suggests otherwise, but it cannot be proven.</li>
  <li>All results are refit from scratch on any new data; there is no live retraining on the web app by design — it serves precomputed results.</li>
</ul>

<h2>9 · Engineering</h2>
<div class="two">
  <div>
    <p><b>One command.</b> <code>python -m pipeline.run</code> reads the six CSVs and writes the scorecard, the attribution, all JSON for the web app, CSV exports, five PDF briefs and this document, in about {{ '%.0f'|format(s.runtime_s) }} seconds (most of it printing PDFs).</p>
    <p><b>Nothing tuned to this dataset.</b> Files are found by their columns, not names; suppliers, materials and thresholds are discovered; every constant lives in <code>config.py</code> with its reasoning. A test runs the full pipeline on a synthetic dataset with different IDs, materials, file names and size, and checks it finds that dataset’s bad suppliers.</p>
  </div>
  <div>
    <p><b>Tested on data with a known answer.</b> <code>python -m evaluation.run</code> generates seven datasets in this schema with planted offenders and the true source of every return — including a 120-supplier panel, suppliers that deteriorate only in the final year, and files as Excel exports them in India (DD/MM/YYYY dates, 12,34,567.89 amounts) — and scores the unchanged pipeline: all planted offenders found in each, the right failure dimension named, rupee figures exact, attribution better than the problem statement’s rule on hidden returns, and no false alarms on a panel where nobody is bad (<code>evaluation/REPORT.md</code>).</p>
    <p><b>Fails loudly.</b> Missing files, mixed units and fanned-out joins stop the run with a specific message. Everything else the pipeline notices ({{ quality.findings | length }} findings on this data) is logged with the action taken and shown on the Method page.</p>
    <p><b>Stack.</b> Python (pandas, scikit-learn, SciPy, Jinja2) for analysis; React + TypeScript + Tailwind, built with Vite, for a static web app that does no computation and cannot go down. An automated test suite guards each claim made here.</p>
  </div>
</div>
</body>
</html>
